# Control lists include a short multilingual title (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08-16): when the product lists controls
such as AC-1, always provide a very short description of what the
control is. That string is multilingual.

## The rule

> **Never show a bare control identifier in an operator list, grid,
> chip, or sentence. Always render `{id} — {short title}` using the
> active locale catalog. Official NIST SP 800-53 short titles are the
> English source. Localized catalogs overlay those titles; the
> identifier stays Latin.**

## Required pattern

Use `formatControlLabel` / `formatControlLabelList` from
`pim-offline-server/ui/src/utils/controlLabel.ts` (or the API `title`
field already localized by the server). Do not concatenate ids with
commas and leave the meaning off.

```tsx
formatControlLabel('AC-1', tControl)
// AC-1 — Policy and Procedures
```

English source: `ui/src/i18n/controlTitles.en.json` (official NIST
short titles, US Government work). Overlays: `ui/src/i18n/locales/<tag>/controls.json`.
New titles land in every shipped content language in the same change
set ([`compliance-artifacts-must-localize.mdc`](compliance-artifacts-must-localize.mdc)).

Enhancements such as `AC-2(1)` use the base title plus
`(enhancement 1)`.

## Forbidden

- A Control column that shows only `AC-1`
- Hardcoded English titles in TSX instead of the `controls` namespace
- Inventing unofficial control body text and calling it the control
- Claiming a control is Met because a title is shown
  ([`compliance-claims-honesty.mdc`](compliance-claims-honesty.mdc))

## Origin

2026-08-16 — operator: Control State History and every other control
list must show a short multilingual description next to the id.
